WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
A synthesis question gives you a target and

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.160
a short list of things you're allowed to

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.160
start from.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.440
The four moves from the last video still

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.920
work: name and count, work backwards, choose, then

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.680
write forwards and check.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.239
But exam questions hide their traps in the

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.600
choice: two starting materials that look almost the

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.920
same, or a route that puts a group

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.080
on the wrong carbon and makes an isomer

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.920
of the target instead.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.800
Here are three HSC-style questions.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.040
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:31.920
through it together.

00:00:33.134 --> 00:00:34.293
Question one.

00:00:34.574 --> 00:00:37.933
A chemist needs hexan-3-one, and can start from

00:00:38.054 --> 00:00:40.893
either hex-1-ene or hex-3-ene.

00:00:41.454 --> 00:00:44.333
Explain which starting material should be used.

00:00:44.654 --> 00:00:47.133
Then construct a flow chart for a two-step

00:00:47.214 --> 00:00:50.493
synthesis, with the reagents and conditions, and an

00:00:50.574 --> 00:00:52.013
equation for each step.

00:00:52.574 --> 00:00:53.933
Pause here to answer.

00:01:04.334 --> 00:01:05.213
Count first.

00:01:05.774 --> 00:01:08.413
Both alkenes have six carbons, and so does

00:01:08.494 --> 00:01:10.973
the target, so the chain keeps its length.

00:01:11.534 --> 00:01:13.613
The target's C double bond O is on

00:01:13.694 --> 00:01:14.653
carbon three.

00:01:15.294 --> 00:01:17.613
Oxidation turns an O H into a C

00:01:17.694 --> 00:01:20.093
double bond O on the same carbon, so

00:01:20.174 --> 00:01:21.853
you need hexan-3-ol.

00:01:22.494 --> 00:01:24.053
Now look at the double bonds.

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:27.613
Hex-3-ene is symmetrical: whichever carbon the O H

00:01:27.694 --> 00:01:29.373
joins, it's carbon three.

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:31.373
Hex-1-ene isn't.

00:01:31.854 --> 00:01:34.333
Water adds to it mainly on carbon two,

00:01:34.414 --> 00:01:36.733
so it gives you mainly the wrong alcohol.

00:01:37.934 --> 00:01:39.773
So use hex-3-ene.

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:40.893
Step one:

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:44.733
water with a dilute acid catalyst, heated, gives

00:01:44.814 --> 00:01:46.253
hexan-3-ol.

00:01:46.334 --> 00:01:48.493
That's an equilibrium, so it gets a double

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:48.973
arrow.

00:01:49.374 --> 00:01:54.253
Step two: acidified potassium dichromate, heated under reflux,

00:01:54.334 --> 00:01:58.093
oxidises that secondary alcohol to hexan-3-one, and the

00:01:58.174 --> 00:01:59.733
orange turns green.

00:02:00.014 --> 00:02:03.453
A ketone isn't oxidised any further by dichromate,

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:05.173
so the product stays put.

00:02:05.454 --> 00:02:08.253
For each equation, write the oxidant as O

00:02:08.334 --> 00:02:10.573
in square brackets, and check that the atoms

00:02:10.654 --> 00:02:11.293
balance.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.780
The classic slip is picking hex-1-ene anyway.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.940
Its alcohol is mainly hexan-2-ol, and oxidation leaves

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.860
the C double bond O where the O

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:22.660
H was.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.660
So you'd make hexan-2-one: the same formula, but

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.940
a different compound.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.100
It's an isomer of the target.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.180
Question two.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:35.700
A chemist has only ethane as a carbon-containing

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.060
starting material.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.340
Construct a flow chart to make ethyl ethanoate,

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.620
naming each intermediate, with the reagents and conditions

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.100
on every arrow.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:48.260
Then explain why the overall yield is expected

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:48.980
to be low.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:50.900
Pause here to answer.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.540
Work backwards.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.220
Cut the ester at the single-bonded oxygen.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.580
The ethanoate part needs ethanoic acid, and the

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.260
ethyl part needs ethanol.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.420
Ethanoic acid comes from oxidising ethanol too, so

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.900
both halves come from one alcohol.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.140
And where does that ethanol come from?

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.940
No reaction we've met turns ethane straight into

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.860
an alcohol, so go through a haloalkane: chloroethane

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:26.500
first.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.420
Now write it forwards.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.340
Ethane and chlorine in U V light give

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.620
chloroethane, as part of a mixture, so separate

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:36.340
it out.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.780
Heat the chloroethane under reflux with aqueous sodium

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.260
hydroxide to get ethanol.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.380
Then split the ethanol into two portions.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.980
Reflux one with excess acidified dichromate to make

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.420
ethanoic acid.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.660
Finally, heat that acid with the rest of

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:54.980
the ethanol and a few drops of concentrated

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.460
sulfuric acid, under reflux.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.620
That's an equilibrium, so it gets a double

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:00.260
arrow.

00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:02.186
Part b.

00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:04.426
Why is the yield low?

00:04:04.747 --> 00:04:07.546
U V substitution doesn't stop neatly at one

00:04:07.627 --> 00:04:10.666
chlorine, so only part of the ethane becomes

00:04:10.747 --> 00:04:11.866
chloroethane.

00:04:12.107 --> 00:04:15.306
Esterification is an equilibrium, so it doesn't go

00:04:15.387 --> 00:04:16.306
to completion.

00:04:16.507 --> 00:04:19.546
And some product is lost at several stages,

00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:22.986
so mixtures, an incomplete last step and losses

00:04:23.227 --> 00:04:24.226
all add up.

00:04:24.507 --> 00:04:26.666
A long route from a cheap starting material

00:04:26.747 --> 00:04:28.986
can still give you very little ester.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.760
The classic slip is oxidising all of the

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:33.560
ethanol.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.720
Then there's no alcohol left to join to

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:36.680
the acid.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.240
The ester needs both halves, so keep a

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.480
portion back before you oxidise.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:43.900
Question three.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.300
A student planned this flow chart to make

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:50.460
propyl butanoate from but-1-ene and 1-chloropropane.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.660
1-chlorobutane was also available.

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:56.780
Identify two errors in the plan, and explain

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:58.060
the effect of each.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.820
Then construct a corrected flow chart.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.380
Pause here to answer.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.520
Check every box, not just the arrows.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.760
The plan says water on but-1-ene gives butan-1-ol.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.520
It gives mainly butan-2-ol instead, because the double

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.400
bond isn't symmetrical and the O H goes

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.080
mainly onto carbon two.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.760
That's an isomer, and a secondary alcohol, so

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.720
oxidising it gives butanone, a ketone.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.600
No arrow leaves a ketone, so the main

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.760
route dead-ends before the acid.

00:05:40.967 --> 00:05:43.926
Second error, and it's separate from the first:

00:05:43.967 --> 00:05:46.246
the oxidation arrow says distil.

00:05:46.807 --> 00:05:50.006
Suppose that box really did hold butan-1-ol.

00:05:50.247 --> 00:05:53.606
Distilling would remove the aldehyde, butanal, as soon

00:05:53.647 --> 00:05:55.526
as it formed, so it would never be

00:05:55.687 --> 00:05:57.246
oxidised on to the acid.

00:05:57.527 --> 00:06:00.646
To make butanoic acid, use excess oxidant and

00:06:00.807 --> 00:06:01.926
heat under reflux.

00:06:02.407 --> 00:06:04.766
The condenser sends the butanal back into the

00:06:04.807 --> 00:06:07.606
flask, so it keeps meeting the oxidant until

00:06:07.687 --> 00:06:08.886
it becomes the acid.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.660
So start from 1-chlorobutane instead.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.340
Aqueous sodium hydroxide under reflux puts the O

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.980
H on carbon one, the same carbon as

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.540
the chlorine, giving butan-1-ol.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:25.300
Excess acidified dichromate under reflux takes it all

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.140
the way to butanoic acid.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:31.620
The other arm is fine: 1-chloropropane gives propan-1-ol.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:34.660
Then esterify, with a double arrow, to make

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.180
propyl butanoate.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:40.640
The classic slip is fixing only the arrow.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:43.720
Keep but-1-ene, switch to reflux, and you still

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.360
make butanone, not the acid.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.520
A wrong box can hide behind a right

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:49.160
arrow.

00:06:50.367 --> 00:06:53.646
So for any synthesis question: count the carbons,

00:06:53.727 --> 00:06:55.886
and find which carbon each group has to

00:06:55.967 --> 00:06:56.646
sit on.

00:06:56.927 --> 00:06:59.086
Choose the start that puts it there, such

00:06:59.167 --> 00:07:02.046
as a symmetrical alkene, or a haloalkane with

00:07:02.127 --> 00:07:03.806
its halogen already in place.

00:07:04.447 --> 00:07:07.486
If one starting material plays two roles, split

00:07:07.567 --> 00:07:07.806
it.

00:07:08.447 --> 00:07:10.926
Write every arrow with its conditions, and a

00:07:11.007 --> 00:07:12.926
double arrow for each equilibrium.

00:07:13.407 --> 00:07:16.606
Then check every box and every arrow, because

00:07:16.647 --> 00:07:18.526
a group on the wrong carbon makes an

00:07:18.607 --> 00:07:20.286
isomer, not the target.

00:07:21.087 --> 00:07:24.926
Next, polymers: how small molecules join into long

00:07:25.007 --> 00:07:25.806
chains.

00:07:27.034 --> 00:07:28.473
Here are the key notes.

00:07:28.714 --> 00:07:30.633
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:30.714 --> 00:07:31.513
to copy them.
